#{[17592186290430 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${\\mathbf{n}}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/f2/f283fe675e0a9ef736947e404f03ab5afb90a4209ba1a6949d18ac6d3fb20048.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=\\mathbf{i},$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=18.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=\\mathbf{j},$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+3\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=54.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 18+2\\cdot 54=144.\n$$"] [17592186290431 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${\\mathbf{n}}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/86/867ab8337894fa75d010bcb12557702059a4a93aaa2bdef2f853c46ff2801d67.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=\\mathbf{i},$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=36.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=\\mathbf{j},$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+3\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=-18.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 36+2\\cdot (-18)=36.\n$$"] [17592186290432 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${\\mathbf{n}}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/00/00a9df76d0be8ffd33b2ee968b57a21eb1e6947af6e94505313f163b584f5769.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=\\mathbf{i},$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=8.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=\\mathbf{j},$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=-12.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 8+2\\cdot (-12)=-8.\n$$"] [17592186290434 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${\\mathbf{n}}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/511bae61d710c3362bb18618717b3d44ef5dcedece5f669e0b5237b7b524a054.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=\\mathbf{i},$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=3i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}3\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=-12.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=\\mathbf{j},$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+2\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}2\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=36.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s=2\\cdot (-12)+2\\cdot 36=48.\n$$"] [17592186290435 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${\\mathbf{n}}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/29/29f4b57751e27f01539fb48acf115b6e255d47a04ee647ef85aa496731b266c8.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=\\mathbf{i},$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+5y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=-8.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=\\mathbf{j},$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(-2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+5y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+2\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & =5{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=20.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s=2\\cdot (-8)+2\\cdot 20=24.\n$$"] [17592186290486 "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 15+0\\cdot \\sqrt{2}+5\\cdot x \\\\  & =5x-30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ 5x-30 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =30 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186290487 "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4+x\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =14-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ 14-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =14 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186290488 "Since $\\mathbf{a}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{b}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}⊥\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot x+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =0 \\\\ 2-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186290489 "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =1\\cdot x+(-3)\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ x-6 & =0 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186290490 "Since $\\mathbf{a}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{b}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}⊥\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-7)+(-4)\\cdot x+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4x-32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =0 \\\\ -4x-32 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =-32 \\\\ x & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186290491 "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3+0\\cdot 0+3\\cdot x \\\\  & =3x+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ 3x+6 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-6 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186290492 "Since $\\mathbf{c}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{d}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}⊥\\mathbf{d}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot \\mathbf{d}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot \\mathbf{d} & ={c}_{x}\\cdot {d}_{x}+{c}_{y}\\cdot {d}_{y}+{c}_{z}\\cdot {d}_{z} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot x+\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0+1\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-3x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot \\mathbf{d} & =0 \\\\ -3x-12 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-12 \\\\ x & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186290493 "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-3)+5\\cdot 1+x\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ 2x-4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292813 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-((-4)\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{j}+((-4)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+8\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4,-4,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292814 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-((-2)\\cdot (-2)-(-4)\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{j}+((-2)\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}-8\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,-4,-8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{2}=\\sqrt{21}.\n$$"] [17592186292815 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k})-(-2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(-2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{i}-((-1)\\cdot 3-2\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{j}+((-1)\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,-3,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186292816 "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k})-(-2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j})-(-2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}-6\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{44} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{2}=\\sqrt{11}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\sqrt{11} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{11} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{22}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292825 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292826 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{13}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292827 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{c}\\cdot \\mathbf{d}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{d}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{d}_{x}^{2}+{d}_{y}^{2}+{d}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292828 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{c}\\cdot \\mathbf{d}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{d}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{d}_{x}^{2}+{d}_{y}^{2}+{d}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{c}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{d} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{c}\\cdot \\mathbf{d}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{c}_{x}^{2}+{c}_{y}^{2}+{c}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}-{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{c}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{d} & =\\frac{5}{3}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292829 "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot x+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-2+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ -2+2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292830 "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot x+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2x+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ 2x+8 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-8 \\\\ x & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297464 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(5\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-((-3)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{j}+((-3)\\cdot (-1)-5\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =10\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}-17\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨10,6,-17⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{10}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-17)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100+36+289} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{425} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297465 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot 3-0\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =0\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,-4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297466 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\times \\mathbf{q} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 0 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(5\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-(0\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 6)\\mathbf{j}+(0\\cdot 0-5\\cdot 6)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =5\\mathbf{i}-6\\mathbf{j}-30\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨5,-6,-30⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}\\times \\mathbf{q}\\hspace{0.167em}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-6)^{2}+(-30)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+36+900} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{961} \\\\  & =31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{31}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186297467 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =8\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+10\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨8,-2,10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{10}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+4+100} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{168} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{42}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{42}}{2}=\\sqrt{42}.\n$$"] [17592186297468 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =\\mathbf{e}-\\mathbf{d} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{j})-(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =\\mathbf{f}-\\mathbf{d} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k})-(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4,1,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-4)-(-3)\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot (-4)-(-3)\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =11\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+10\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨11,-4,10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DF}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{11}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{10}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{121+16+100} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{237}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,DEF$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{237}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186297469 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =\\mathbf{m}-\\mathbf{l} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k})-(-3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,2,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{l} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j})-(-3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =5\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨5,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 3 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1)-(-3)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-(3\\cdot (-1)-(-3)\\cdot 5)\\mathbf{j}+(3\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 5)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-5\\mathbf{i}-12\\mathbf{j}-13\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-5,-12,-13⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-12)^{2}+(-13)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+144+169} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{338} \\\\  & =13\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{13\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186297473 "Note that the length of the altitude $NP$ can be related to the area of $△\\,LMN$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,NP\\cdot LM.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $NP,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =\\mathbf{m}-\\mathbf{l} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{l} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-5\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-5,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{i}-((-3)\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot (-5))\\mathbf{j}+((-3)\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-5))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-5\\mathbf{i}-8\\mathbf{j}+9\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-5,-8,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+{9}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{170}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $LM$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LM & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,NP\\cdot LM,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,NP\\cdot LM \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,NP\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ \\sqrt{170} & =NP\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ NP & =\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{\\sqrt{19}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3230}}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297474 "Note that the length of the altitude $UT$ can be related to the area of $△\\,UVW$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,UT\\cdot VW.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $UT,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{VW}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{VU}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{VW} & =\\mathbf{w}-\\mathbf{v} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,1,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{VU} & =\\mathbf{u}-\\mathbf{v} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j})-(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{VW}\\times \\overset{\\to }{VU} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =7\\mathbf{i}+13\\mathbf{j}+9\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨7,13,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{VW}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{VU}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{VW}\\times \\overset{\\to }{VU}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+{13}^{2}+{9}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{299}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,UVW$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{299}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $VW$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}VW & =|\\overset{\\to }{VW}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,UT\\cdot VW,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,UT\\cdot VW \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{299}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,UT\\cdot \\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{299} & =UT\\cdot \\sqrt{14} \\\\ UT & =\\frac{\\sqrt{299}}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4186}}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297475 "Note that the length of the altitude $VL$ can be related to the area of $△\\,TUV$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VL\\cdot TU\n$$\n\nSo, to find $VL,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU} & =\\mathbf{u}-\\mathbf{t} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =\\mathbf{v}-\\mathbf{t} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =0\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}+6\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,6,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{72} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,TUV$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{2}=3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $TU$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TU & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VL\\cdot TU,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}VL\\cdot TU \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}VL\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =VL\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ VL & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ VL & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297477 "Note that the length of the altitude $IP$ can be related to the area of $△\\,HIJ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}IP\\cdot HJ\n$$\n\nSo, to find $IP,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{HJ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{HI}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{HJ} & =\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{h} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,-4,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{HI} & =\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{h} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{HJ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{HI} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-4)\\cdot 3-1\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot (-1)-(-4)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-11\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-11,-1,7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{HJ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{HI}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{HJ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{HI}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-11)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{171} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,HIJ$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{19}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $HJ$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}HJ & =|\\overset{\\to }{HJ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}IP\\cdot HJ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}IP\\cdot HJ \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{19}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,IP\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{19} & =\\sqrt{2}\\,IP \\\\ IP & =\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{38}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297486 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =\\mathbf{e}-\\mathbf{d} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =\\mathbf{f}-\\mathbf{d} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-(3\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{j}+(3\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-5\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-5,-4,7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DF}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{90} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,DEF$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186297487 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =\\mathbf{m}-\\mathbf{l} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j})-(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{l} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 4-1\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =7\\mathbf{i}-9\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨7,-9,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+(-9)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{146}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{146}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186297489 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{ST}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{SU}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ST} & =\\mathbf{t}-\\mathbf{s} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{SU} & =\\mathbf{u}-\\mathbf{s} \\\\  & =(4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{i}+0\\mathbf{j}+0\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ST}\\times \\overset{\\to }{SU} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =0\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}-8\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,-4,-8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{ST}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{SU}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{ST}\\times \\overset{\\to }{SU}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,STU$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{2}=2\\sqrt{5}.\n$$"] [17592186297491 "Note that the length of the altitude $CK$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CK\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CK,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(4\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+0\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{i}-(3\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{j}+(3\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{62}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CK\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CK\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ CK & =\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ CK & =\\frac{\\sqrt{806}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297492 "Note that the length of the altitude $HK$ can be related to the area of $△\\,GHI$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}HK\\cdot GI\n$$\n\nSo, to find $HK,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{GI}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{GH}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{GI} & =\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{g} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{GH} & =\\mathbf{h}-\\mathbf{g} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{GI}\\times \\overset{\\to }{GH} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =8\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-5\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨8,-1,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{GI}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{GH}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{GI}\\times \\overset{\\to }{GH}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,GHI$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $GI$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}GI & =|\\overset{\\to }{GI}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}HK\\cdot GI,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}HK\\cdot \\sqrt{11} \\\\ HK & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ HK & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{110}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297493 "Note that the length of the altitude $TK$ can be related to the area of $△\\,RST$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}TK\\cdot RS\n$$\n\nSo, to find $TK,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{RS}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RS} & =\\mathbf{s}-\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =(7\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k})-(4\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}+6\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{RT} & =\\mathbf{t}-\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =(6\\mathbf{i}+8\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(4\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}+6\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RS}\\times \\overset{\\to }{RT} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 3 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)(-2)-(-1)\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{i}-(3(-2)-(-1)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+(3\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =5\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+11\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨5,4,11⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{RS}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{RS}\\times \\overset{\\to }{RT}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{11}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{162} \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,RST$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $RS$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RS & =|\\overset{\\to }{RS}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}TK\\cdot RS,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}TK\\cdot \\sqrt{11} \\\\ TK & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ TK & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{22}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299657 "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =10+4 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299658 "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v} & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =24-12 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299660 "We know that $\\mathbf{c}⊥\\mathbf{d}$ ($\\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ are perpendicular) if and only if $\\mathbf{c}\\cdot \\mathbf{d}=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot \\mathbf{d} & ={c}_{x}\\cdot {d}_{x}+{c}_{y}\\cdot {d}_{y}+{c}_{z}\\cdot {d}_{z} \\\\  & =k\\cdot (-3)+2k\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-3k+4k+8 \\\\  & =k+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot \\mathbf{d} & =0 \\\\ k+8 & =0 \\\\ k & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299664 "We know that $\\mathbf{a}⊥\\mathbf{b}$ ($\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are perpendicular) if and only if $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}\\cdot 8+(-4)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =16{x}^{2}-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =0 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ 16{x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are told that $x>0.$ Therefore, $x=1.$"] [17592186299701 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{l}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299702 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{s}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =10cos\\hspace{-0.167em}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299703 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =8cos\\hspace{-0.167em}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299704 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{u}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{t} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{t}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}cos\\hspace{-0.167em}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299705 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{s}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}cos\\hspace{-0.167em}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299706 "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4)(1)+(-1)(2)+(1)(2)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-2+2}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299707 "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)(2)+(-2)(1)+(1)(-2)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-2-2}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299708 "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{s}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{s}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{s}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{r}_{x}{s}_{x}+{r}_{y}{s}_{y}+{r}_{z}{s}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{s}_{x}^{2}+{s}_{y}^{2}+{s}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)(2)+(2)(0)+(3)(0)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299709 "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{n}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{m} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{m}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{m}_{x}{n}_{x}+{m}_{y}{n}_{y}+{m}_{z}{n}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{n}_{x}^{2}+{n}_{y}^{2}+{n}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6)(0)+(8)(5)+(0)(12)}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{12}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{\\sqrt{169}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{m}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{m}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{m}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{m}_{x}{n}_{x}+{m}_{y}{n}_{y}+{m}_{z}{n}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{m}_{x}^{2}+{m}_{y}^{2}+{m}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{8}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{n}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{m}-{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{m}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{40}{13}-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{40-52}{13} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299710 "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(9)(0)+(12)(3)+(0)(4)}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{p}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{p}_{x}^{2}+{p}_{y}^{2}+{p}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\sqrt{{9}^{2}+{12}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\sqrt{225}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}-{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{p}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q} & =\\frac{36}{5}-\\frac{12}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299726 "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =3\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ 3x+1 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299731 "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+1\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299732 "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =-2+6+4x \\\\  & =4x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ 4x+4 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299736 "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =2\\cdot x+(-3)\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ 2x-12 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =12 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299737 "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 5+2\\cdot x+(-3)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-20+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ -20+2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =20 \\\\ x & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299738 "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-6+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ -6+2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299739 "Since $\\mathbf{a}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{b}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}⊥\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =3\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =0 \\\\ 3x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299740 "Since $\\mathbf{a}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{b}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}⊥\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & ={a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 3+5\\cdot x+1\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =5x-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-10 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =10 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323794 "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point  where $(x,y)=(1,1)$, can be found by computing $∇g(1,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & ={g}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{g}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot y\\,i+\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot x\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j \\\\ ∇g(1,1) & =\\frac{1}{(1)}\\,i+\\frac{1}{(1)}\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇g(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{∇}g(1,1)}{‖\\mathrm{∇}g(1,1)‖} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323795 "First, we bring all variables to one side: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy+6{y}^{2} & =x-2 \\\\ 2xy+6{y}^{2}-x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2xy+6{y}^{2}-x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-2.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(8,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(8,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(8,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+6{y}^{2}-x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+6{y}^{2}-x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2y-1)\\,i+(2x+12y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(8,-3) & =(2(-3)-1)\\,i+(2(8)+12(-3))\\,j \\\\  & =-7\\,i-20\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-7,-20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Hence, $∇f(8,-3)=⟨-7,-20⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(8,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-8}{-7} & =\\frac{y-(-3)}{-20} \\\\ -20(x-8) & =-7(y+3) \\\\ -20x+7y+160+21 & =0 \\\\ -20x+7y+181 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323861 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot y+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y) & ={g}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{g}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+(\\frac{1}{y}-2y\\operatorname{sin}({y}^{2}))\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323918 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =8xy+2-8e \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy & =2-8e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=2-8e.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,e)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(1,e)={f}_{y}(1,e)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(1,e)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x}{y}-8x)i-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8y)\\,j \\\\ t(1,e) & =(\\frac{2(1)}{e}-8(1))i-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-8(e))\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $(\\frac{2}{e}-8)\\mathbf{i}+(8e-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [17592186326759 "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t(3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-(\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(1+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=1+2t$ and $y(t)=2+2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+y\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+2t)\\cdot 2+(2+2t)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6+8t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[6t+4{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[6(1)+4(1)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186326775 "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the line segment from $A$ to $B.$  It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩+t(⟨1,2⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\  & =⟨t,1+t⟩ \\\\  & =t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=t$ and $y=1+t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}({\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)) & =(2x-3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(2t-3(1+t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(t+(1+t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(-3-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{1}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}({\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}_{1}^{'}(t) & =((-3-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-3-t+2t+1 \\\\  & =t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{F}({\\mathbf{r}}_{\\mathbf{1}}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}_{1}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-2t]}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}-2-0]+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186326776 "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) &=x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}={t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{xy}\\,dx+dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4y{e}^{xy}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4y{e}^{xy}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4t\\cdot {e}^{{t}^{3}\\cdot t}\\cdot 3{t}^{2}+1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12{t}^{3}{e}^{{t}^{4}}+1\\,dt \\\\  & ={[3{e}^{{t}^{4}}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[3{e}^{(1)^{4}}+(1)]-[3{e}^{(0)^{4}}+(0)] \\\\  & =[3e+1]-[3] \\\\  & =3e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186335800 "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g\\,∇\\,f+f\\,∇\\,g\n$$\n\nSo, using this product rule we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)+f(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}(xy))(y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+xy(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =[y\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}]+[x\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}] \\\\  & =y(\\operatorname{ln}(xy)+1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x(\\operatorname{ln}(xy)+1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186335802 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0+0}{3\\cdot 0+0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x+x}{3x+x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x}{4x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=-x$” is true. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=-x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-2x+x}{3x-x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-x}{2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $0$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186335804 "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-1,1,-1).$  \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$  and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,k \\\\  & =({y}^{2}+2xz)\\text{ }i+(2xy+{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+(2yz+{x}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,1,-1) & =((1)^{2}+2(-1)(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)(1)+(-1)^{2}))\\,j+(2(1)(-1)+(-1)^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i-1j-1\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1).$"] [17592186338894 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX} & =\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-4,4,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =\\mathbf{y}-\\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -4 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{i}-((-4)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{j}+((-4)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =6\\mathbf{i}+9\\mathbf{j}-12\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨6,9,-12⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{9}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{261}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,WXY$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{261}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186338895 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{NP}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{NQ}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{NP} & =\\mathbf{p}-\\mathbf{n} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{NQ} & =\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{n} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{NP}\\times \\overset{\\to }{NQ} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{NP}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{NQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{NP}\\times \\overset{\\to }{NQ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,NPQ$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186338896 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{FG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{FH}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{FG} & =\\mathbf{g}-\\mathbf{f} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{FH} & =\\mathbf{h}-\\mathbf{f} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{FG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{FH} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{FG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{FH}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{FG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{FH}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,FGH$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186338898 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{CE}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{c} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{CE} & =\\mathbf{e}-\\mathbf{c} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{CE} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-(0\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{j}+(0\\cdot 3-0\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{CE}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{CD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{CE}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,CDE$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=1.\n$$"] [17592186338899 "Note that the length of the altitude $MH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,LMN$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN\n$$\n\nSo, to find $MH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =\\mathbf{m}-\\mathbf{l} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{l} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{j}+((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $LN$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =|\\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & =MH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ MH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ MH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338900 "Note that the length of the altitude $VH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,TUV$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU\n$$\n\nSo, to find $VH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU} & =\\mathbf{u}-\\mathbf{t} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j}-(3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,-1,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =\\mathbf{v}-\\mathbf{t} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{i}-((-3)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{j}+((-3)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,TUV$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $TU$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TU & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & =VH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ VH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{19}} \\\\ VH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{38}}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338901 "Note that the length of the altitude $ZH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,WYZ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot WY\n$$\n\nSo, to find $ZH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WZ}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WY} & =\\mathbf{y}-\\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{WZ} & =\\mathbf{z}-\\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,-4,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WY}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WZ} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-4)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+((-1)\\cdot (-4)-(-1)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WZ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{WY}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WZ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,WYZ$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $WY$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}WY & =|\\overset{\\to }{WY}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot WY,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot WY \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =ZH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ ZH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ ZH & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338902 "Note that the length of the altitude $GH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,GSM$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot SM\n$$\n\nSo, to find $GH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{SG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{SM}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SG} & =\\mathbf{g}-\\mathbf{s} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-3,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{SM} & =\\mathbf{m}-\\mathbf{s} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{SM} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-((-3)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{j}+((-3)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-6\\mathbf{j}-6\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-6,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{SG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{SM}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{SG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{SM}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-6)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{76} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,GSM$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{19}}{2}=\\sqrt{19}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $SM$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}SM & =|\\overset{\\to }{SM}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot SM,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot SM \\\\ \\sqrt{19} & =\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{19} & =GH\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ GH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ GH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{38}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338904 "Note that the length of the altitude $DH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,DMA$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot MA\n$$\n\nSo, to find $DH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{MD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{MA}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{m} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{MA} & =\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{m} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{MA} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{i}-(0\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+(0\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =8\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨8,4,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{MD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{MA}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{MD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{MA}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{96} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,DMA$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{4\\sqrt{6}}{2}=2\\sqrt{6}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $MA$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}MA & =|\\overset{\\to }{MA}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot MA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot MA \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6} & =\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6} & =DH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ DH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ DH & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
